joint_state_publisher_gui: generate sliders to mimike physical motors and broadcast fake joint state.
robot_state_publisher : constantly listening to URDF file and the joint states coming from the GUI/real motor to perform a *forward Kinematics* then publish the final 3D coordinates to /robot_description.

# URDF
1. Link tags
<link name="arm_link">
    <visual>
        <geometry>
        <origin>
        <material>
    </visual>

    <collision>
        <geometry>
        <origin>
    </collision>

    <inertial>
        <mass>
        <origin>
        <inertia>
    </inertial>
</link>
2. Joint tags
*Types* : fixed, primastc, revolute and continuous
<joint name="arm_joint" type="revolute">
    <parent link="slider_link">
    <child link="arm_link">
    <origin xyz="0.25 0 0.15" rpy="0 0 0">
    <axis xyz="0 -1 0">
    <limit lower="0" upper="${pi/2}" velocity="100" effort="100">
</joint>

We create a different package for the URDF with ament_cmake intead of ament_python because here! Even though you are writing Python nodes for your logic, description packages are almost always built with CMake
``ros2 pkg create --build-type ament_cmake otto_description``
``cd otto_description``
``mkdir urdf meshes launch rviz``

otto_ws/src/otto_description/
├── CMakeLists.txt      # Build instructions (tells ROS to copy these folders)
├── package.xml         # Package dependencies (depends on urdf, xacro)
├── launch/
│   └── display.launch.py  # The launch file to open RViz and Robot State Publisher
├── meshes/
│   └── (empty for now)    # Where you will drop your 3D CAD files (.stl or .dae) later
├── rviz/
│   └── urdf_config.rviz   # Saves your RViz camera angles and background colors
└── urdf/
    ├── otto.urdf.xacro    # <-- THIS is where your master URDF file goes!
    └── otto_core.xacro    # (Optional) Sub-files if you break the URDF into pieces


# How to run Robot_description package
1. Build the package with colcon.
2. Launch the robot_state_publisher launch file with ros2 launch otto_description otto.launch.py.
3. Launch joint_state_publisher_gui with ros2 run joint_state_publisher_gui joint_state_publisher_gui. 
4. Launch RViz with rviz2

To replicate the RViz display shown in the video you will want to

Set your fixed frame to world
Add a RobotModel display, with the topic set to /robot_description, and alpha set to 0.8
Add a TF display with names enabled.

# URDF Modeling
1. Draw on a paper to get an idea of what I want to build, with dimensions.
When hand designing the model before the urdf modeling, it helpful the know the dimensions & specifications of each component.
2. Identify joint points in the model
3. I identify parent and child relationship: The Rule of Thumb: If I hold part A still, and move part B, part B is the child.
4. In the urdf modeling, always start from the base_link. And each link is automatically follow by a joint tag.
Joint tag always has as name the child name

After have modelize the the robot using boxes, to find the correct joint points and their rotations, you have to take your time to find
